WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.224 --> 00:00:01.309
- Good morning, Believe Nation.

00:00:01.309 --> 00:00:02.692
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:02.692 --> 00:00:05.517
My one word is believe, and
I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:05.517 --> 00:00:08.280
will solve all of the
worlds major problems.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.647
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:09.647 --> 00:00:13.111
today's message is outdo yourself.

00:00:13.111 --> 00:00:14.795
Over to you, Simon Sinek.

00:00:14.795 --> 00:00:16.947
(rooster crows)

00:00:16.947 --> 00:00:19.672
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:19.672 --> 00:00:22.546
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:00:22.546 --> 00:00:26.488
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:26.488 --> 00:00:28.811
- I spoke at an education
summit for Microsoft.

00:00:28.811 --> 00:00:32.644
I also spoke at an
education summit for Apple.

00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:35.854
At the education summit for Microsoft,

00:00:35.854 --> 00:00:38.293
I would say that 70% of the executives

00:00:38.293 --> 00:00:41.405
spent about 70% of their presentations

00:00:41.405 --> 00:00:44.072
talking about how to beat Apple.

00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:48.079
At the Apple education summit,

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.961
100% of the executives spent
100% of their presentations

00:00:52.961 --> 00:00:54.830
talking about how to help teachers teach,

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.787
and how to help students learn.

00:00:56.787 --> 00:00:58.464
One is playing this way,

00:00:58.464 --> 00:01:01.537
and one is playing that way.

00:01:01.537 --> 00:01:03.350
One is playing finite,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:04.854
and the other one is infinite.

00:01:04.854 --> 00:01:06.876
Guess which one gets frustrated.

00:01:06.876 --> 00:01:09.420
(audience laughs)

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.097
So at the end of my talk at Microsoft,

00:01:11.097 --> 00:01:12.288
they gave me a gift.

00:01:12.288 --> 00:01:14.699
They gave me the new
Zune when it was a thing.

00:01:14.699 --> 00:01:17.532
(audience laughs)

00:01:19.519 --> 00:01:23.481
And let me tell you, this
thing was spectacular.

00:01:23.481 --> 00:01:25.987
It was the most elegant piece
of technology I'd ever used.

00:01:25.987 --> 00:01:28.552
The user interface was incredible.

00:01:28.552 --> 00:01:30.507
The design was spectacular.

00:01:30.507 --> 00:01:32.927
I absolutely loved it.

00:01:32.927 --> 00:01:35.660
It was easy to use, and it was bright,

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.014
and gorgeous and fanta,
it didn't work on iTunes,

00:01:38.014 --> 00:01:39.083
which is a different problem.

00:01:39.083 --> 00:01:40.621
So I couldn't use it,

00:01:40.621 --> 00:01:41.454
but it was amazing.

00:01:41.454 --> 00:01:43.461
(audience laughs)

00:01:43.461 --> 00:01:46.331
And elegant, my God, it was elegant.

00:01:46.331 --> 00:01:48.250
So I'm sitting in the
back of a taxi with a

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:49.370
very senior Apple executive,

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.339
sort of employee number 12 kind of guy,

00:01:51.339 --> 00:01:53.838
and you know, I like to stir pots,

00:01:53.838 --> 00:01:55.536
so I turned to him, I said,

00:01:55.536 --> 00:01:56.536
"You know...

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.879
Microsoft gave me their new Zune,

00:02:00.879 --> 00:02:04.796
and it is so much better
than your iPod touch."

00:02:06.948 --> 00:02:11.007
And he turned to me, and
he said, "I have no doubt."

00:02:11.007 --> 00:02:12.507
Conversation over.

00:02:13.473 --> 00:02:16.306
(audience laughs)

00:02:17.362 --> 00:02:18.957
Because the infinite player understands,

00:02:18.957 --> 00:02:21.266
sometimes you're ahead, and
sometimes you're behind.

00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:23.897
Sometimes your product is
better and sometimes it's worse.

00:02:23.897 --> 00:02:26.510
The goal isn't to be the best every day.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.403
The goal isn't to outdo
your competition every day.

00:02:30.403 --> 00:02:32.220
That's a finite construction.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:33.437
If I had said to Microsoft,

00:02:33.437 --> 00:02:35.021
"I've got the new iPod touch
and it's so much better

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:36.440
than your Zune", they would have said,

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.136
"Can we see it, what does it do?"

00:02:38.136 --> 00:02:40.658
React, react, react, react.

00:02:40.658 --> 00:02:45.492
Finite players play to beat
the people around them.

00:02:45.492 --> 00:02:50.395
Infinite players play to
be better than themselves.

00:02:50.395 --> 00:02:51.731
To wake up every single day and say,

00:02:51.731 --> 00:02:53.820
how can we make our
company a better version

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.665
of itself today than it was yesterday?

00:02:56.665 --> 00:02:59.342
How can we create a product
this week that's better

00:02:59.342 --> 00:03:01.310
than the product we created last week?

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.000
We also have to play the infinite game.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.736
It's not about being ranked number one.

00:03:05.736 --> 00:03:07.014
It's not about having more followers

00:03:07.014 --> 00:03:08.939
on Twitter than your friends.

00:03:08.939 --> 00:03:10.985
It's not about outdoing anyone.

00:03:10.985 --> 00:03:13.796
It's about how to outdo yourself.

00:03:13.796 --> 00:03:15.269
It's not about selling more books,

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:17.606
or getting more TED
views than somebody else.

00:03:17.606 --> 00:03:19.580
It's about how to make sure that the work

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.346
that you're producing is better than

00:03:21.346 --> 00:03:24.129
the work you produced before.

00:03:24.129 --> 00:03:26.590
You are your competition.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.381
And that is what ensures you
stay in the game the longest,

00:03:29.381 --> 00:03:33.332
and that is what ensures you find joy.

00:03:33.332 --> 00:03:37.367
Because the joy comes,
not from comparison,

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:39.117
but from advancement.

00:03:39.987 --> 00:03:42.245
- So I think having enemies
is actually a good thing.

00:03:42.245 --> 00:03:44.627
I think enemies can push you to be better,

00:03:44.627 --> 00:03:46.800
to do more, to go stronger.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.531
It doesn't mean you have to copy them,

00:03:48.531 --> 00:03:51.229
but I think they push you to
be a better version of you.

00:03:51.229 --> 00:03:53.540
Tupac was better because of Biggie.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.598
The Yankees are better
because of the Red Sox.

00:03:56.598 --> 00:03:59.606
Apple is better because Microsoft exists.

00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:01.577
I'm better because of people like

00:04:01.577 --> 00:04:04.079
Gary Vaynerchuk and Grant
Cardone, and all these other guys

00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:05.944
who are making YouTube videos as well.

00:04:05.944 --> 00:04:08.679
It doesn't mean that we copy them, right?

00:04:08.679 --> 00:04:10.439
The way the way that Gary wins is through

00:04:10.439 --> 00:04:12.732
his speeches and through his vlogs,

00:04:12.732 --> 00:04:15.421
and daily people following him around.

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.244
If I was just copying him and saying,

00:04:17.244 --> 00:04:20.486
well this is the way to win,
I need to do exactly that,

00:04:20.486 --> 00:04:21.808
then I'm going to lose.

00:04:21.808 --> 00:04:23.515
If you look at your competition and say,

00:04:23.515 --> 00:04:26.026
I'm just going to be a
copycat, that's when you lose.

00:04:26.026 --> 00:04:28.558
Because you can't out-them them, right?

00:04:28.558 --> 00:04:32.742
I can't out-Gary Gary, and
Gary can't out-Evan Evan.

00:04:32.742 --> 00:04:34.529
It's just not possible, right?

00:04:34.529 --> 00:04:36.196
So if you look at your
competition just saying

00:04:36.196 --> 00:04:38.255
I'm a copycat, you've lost already.

00:04:38.255 --> 00:04:40.750
But to look at them and
see they're growing,

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:41.841
they're gaining, you know,

00:04:41.841 --> 00:04:43.710
they're getting more market share,

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.506
they're getting more
customers, they're getting

00:04:45.506 --> 00:04:49.433
more subscribers, that
can push you to do better.

00:04:49.433 --> 00:04:51.831
Sprinters do better when they have

00:04:51.831 --> 00:04:53.941
people behind them who are also good.

00:04:53.941 --> 00:04:56.218
If you're constantly
the best in your field

00:04:56.218 --> 00:04:59.092
and you have no
competition, then it's easy

00:04:59.092 --> 00:05:01.058
to get complacent, it's easy to get lazy,

00:05:01.058 --> 00:05:02.533
it's easy to relax.

00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:04.591
And so, I think having
enemy is a good thing,

00:05:04.591 --> 00:05:06.150
but I definitely agree
with you need to try

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.782
to outdo yourself every single day.

00:05:08.782 --> 00:05:11.420
I look at myself as my
competition, I look at my videos

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.315
from last week and say,
how do I do better,

00:05:13.315 --> 00:05:14.743
how do I get better?

00:05:14.743 --> 00:05:18.039
Yes, I want to aspire
to be like a Les Brown,

00:05:18.039 --> 00:05:20.107
or a Tony Robbins and learn from them,

00:05:20.107 --> 00:05:22.199
and their skills, and their abilities,

00:05:22.199 --> 00:05:23.764
and not be them, but learn from them

00:05:23.764 --> 00:05:27.540
to be a better version of
me, and I want to be better

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.093
than I was last week, and the week before,

00:05:30.093 --> 00:05:31.591
and the week before, and the week before.

00:05:31.591 --> 00:05:34.212
And you may not always see a
difference from week to week.

00:05:34.212 --> 00:05:37.394
But you can definitely see
a difference with more time.

00:05:37.394 --> 00:05:39.375
There's been a lot of people
come back to my videos from

00:05:39.375 --> 00:05:42.890
five, six years ago, and say,
"Wow so much has changed!"

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.497
You know, I'm still the
same guy, I still have

00:05:44.497 --> 00:05:47.450
the same points of view,
but my abilities have grown

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:48.582
because I've practiced.

00:05:48.582 --> 00:05:51.133
We've done 5,000 videos, right?

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:52.437
You got to get good at something

00:05:52.437 --> 00:05:54.777
if you've done it 5,000 times.

00:05:54.777 --> 00:05:57.409
Even if you're practicing
really inefficiently,

00:05:57.409 --> 00:05:59.111
you better get good at anything

00:05:59.111 --> 00:06:01.647
if you're trying it 5,000 times.

00:06:01.647 --> 00:06:04.942
And so, I think it's really important that

00:06:04.942 --> 00:06:06.242
you can look up to other people,

00:06:06.242 --> 00:06:08.196
you can look up to your
rivals potentially,

00:06:08.196 --> 00:06:11.667
and say these people are
pushing me to do better,

00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:14.797
to be more myself, to work
harder, to grind it out more,

00:06:14.797 --> 00:06:16.630
to hustle more, and...

00:06:18.287 --> 00:06:20.222
I'm not going to use

00:06:20.222 --> 00:06:23.945
external people as my
barometer of success.

00:06:23.945 --> 00:06:26.843
Because if I'm only looking at Les Brown,

00:06:26.843 --> 00:06:29.902
I say, well, maybe I'll
never get to Les Brown level.

00:06:29.902 --> 00:06:32.680
Don't use that to beat you
down, use it as a kick forward.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.332
And, I want to compare myself
to how I was last week.

00:06:37.332 --> 00:06:39.229
'Cause if you're only comparing yourself

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:41.922
when you are at your start,
to somebody else's middle

00:06:41.922 --> 00:06:45.057
or somebody else's end,
it's not a fair fight.

00:06:45.057 --> 00:06:48.196
And so you can use that as
inspiration, but tactically,

00:06:48.196 --> 00:06:51.911
and practically, on a day
to day, week to week basis,

00:06:51.911 --> 00:06:54.883
competing and beating
and outdoing yourself

00:06:54.883 --> 00:06:56.126
is super important.

00:06:56.126 --> 00:06:57.713
So I don't think it's one or the other.

00:06:57.713 --> 00:06:59.848
I think you take the
best of both to help you

00:06:59.848 --> 00:07:01.940
become the best version of yourself.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.705
So the question today is, I'm curious,

00:07:03.705 --> 00:07:07.821
what have you done to
recently outdo yourself?

00:07:07.821 --> 00:07:09.655
If you're looking back
a week, a month, a year,

00:07:09.655 --> 00:07:11.642
what have you gotten really good at

00:07:11.642 --> 00:07:13.716
that you weren't so good at before?

00:07:13.716 --> 00:07:17.132
Have a little bit of a brag,
show some love for yourself,

00:07:17.132 --> 00:07:18.665
have a little bravado.

00:07:18.665 --> 00:07:20.992
It's okay this time, I
want to see it come out.

00:07:20.992 --> 00:07:22.848
Leave it down in the comments
below, really curious

00:07:22.848 --> 00:07:23.993
to see what you have to say.

00:07:23.993 --> 00:07:26.557
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Jonathan Balgame.

00:07:26.557 --> 00:07:28.485
Jonathan, thank you so much
for picking up a copy of

00:07:28.485 --> 00:07:30.761
my book, Your One Word, and
taking a picture with it

00:07:30.761 --> 00:07:33.469
and posting it on Twitter,
it was awesome to see,

00:07:33.469 --> 00:07:35.502
and I'm really thankful for your support,

00:07:35.502 --> 00:07:37.602
and I'm glad that you enjoyed my book.

00:07:37.602 --> 00:07:39.098
So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:07:39.098 --> 00:07:40.234
I believe in you.

00:07:40.234 --> 00:07:42.184
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:07:42.184 --> 00:07:45.238
and whatever your one word is, much love,

00:07:45.238 --> 00:07:46.757
and I'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:07:46.757 --> 00:07:49.682
for another shot of entspresso.

00:07:49.682 --> 00:07:52.440
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.026
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:07:56.026 --> 00:07:58.840
(typewriter clacking)

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:02.555
- How many times have you
ever heard the phrase,

00:08:02.555 --> 00:08:05.305
you are perfect, just as you are?

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:08.183
Have you heard that a lot, man?

00:08:08.183 --> 00:08:10.931
You know what, you are
perfect just as you are,

00:08:10.931 --> 00:08:12.797
you are so perfect just as you are.

00:08:12.797 --> 00:08:15.119
Well you know what, you are not.

00:08:15.119 --> 00:08:18.119
You are not perfect just as you are.

00:08:20.064 --> 00:08:23.481
You are perfect, but you're not complete.

00:08:24.631 --> 00:08:26.780
This is the thing, and this
is what I mean by that.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:29.368
A lot of people are going to
go, oh what do you mean, I'm,

00:08:29.368 --> 00:08:31.294
what are you trying to
say, I got to change?

00:08:31.294 --> 00:08:32.800
I got to change into somebody else?

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:33.633
Yes, yes.

00:08:34.721 --> 00:08:35.913
You need to change.

00:08:35.913 --> 00:08:39.342
Everybody, everybody out
there needs to change.

00:08:39.342 --> 00:08:41.141
We are designed for change.

00:08:41.141 --> 00:08:42.764
Nothing in this world stays the same.

00:08:42.764 --> 00:08:45.348
You are either getting better,
or you are getting worse.

00:08:45.348 --> 00:08:46.620
Let me describe something to you.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.087
When I say you are perfect,
but you are not complete,

00:08:51.087 --> 00:08:53.249
let's say this building I'm in.

00:08:53.249 --> 00:08:56.355
If I looked at the
blueprints for this building.

00:08:56.355 --> 00:08:58.268
The blueprints for this building

00:08:58.268 --> 00:09:00.945
before it was built were perfect.

00:09:00.945 --> 00:09:03.824
Everything was in its right
place, everything was great.

00:09:03.824 --> 00:09:05.815
But could I walk in it?

00:09:05.815 --> 00:09:06.768
Could I do anything?

00:09:06.768 --> 00:09:07.839
Did I have a building?

00:09:07.839 --> 00:09:09.256
No, why?

00:09:09.256 --> 00:09:10.695
It wasn't complete.

00:09:10.695 --> 00:09:14.592
When they framed up this
building and put wood, and metal,

00:09:14.592 --> 00:09:17.049
and all this stuff, but
they didn't do the floor,

00:09:17.049 --> 00:09:19.320
didn't do the electrical, was it perfect?

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:20.987
Yes, it was perfect.

00:09:22.364 --> 00:09:24.744
But it wasn't complete.

00:09:24.744 --> 00:09:27.052
You are like this building.

00:09:27.052 --> 00:09:31.911
You are perfect in every way,
but you're just not done.

00:09:31.911 --> 00:09:33.078
You're not done.

00:09:33.078 --> 00:09:33.911
And you know what?

00:09:33.911 --> 00:09:36.883
Anybody's trying to tell
you, don't try to change me.

00:09:36.883 --> 00:09:38.647
Now, no one should try to change you,

00:09:38.647 --> 00:09:41.314
but you have to change yourself.

00:09:42.561 --> 00:09:46.624
You owe it to everyone to change yourself

00:09:46.624 --> 00:09:47.902
into a better person.

00:09:47.902 --> 00:09:49.633
Into a better human being.

00:09:49.633 --> 00:09:51.291
That's what you have to do.

00:09:51.291 --> 00:09:53.329
You are perfect.

00:09:53.329 --> 00:09:55.152
But you're just not complete.

00:09:55.152 --> 00:09:56.669
So, when you look at yourself,

00:09:56.669 --> 00:09:58.505
look at yourself as that building.

00:09:58.505 --> 00:10:00.254
And it's framed up, and everything

00:10:00.254 --> 00:10:03.508
is in it's right place,
but you're just not done.

00:10:03.508 --> 00:10:04.452
I want to tell you that.

00:10:04.452 --> 00:10:07.024
So anybody who's trying to tell you that,

00:10:07.024 --> 00:10:09.793
I'm perfect just the way I am

00:10:09.793 --> 00:10:11.419
is not trying to change.

00:10:11.419 --> 00:10:12.252
And guess what?

00:10:12.252 --> 00:10:13.143
You're not.

00:10:13.143 --> 00:10:14.425
And it's not working.

00:10:14.425 --> 00:10:16.002
You're a half done building.

00:10:16.002 --> 00:10:18.296
Who wants to live in a
building with no roof?

00:10:18.296 --> 00:10:19.552
Nobody.

00:10:19.552 --> 00:10:22.126
And I'm just trying to
break it down to you,

00:10:22.126 --> 00:10:24.119
that you hear these catchphrases.

00:10:24.119 --> 00:10:26.117
You hear this stuff all the time.

00:10:26.117 --> 00:10:28.115
And everybody just takes it and says,

00:10:28.115 --> 00:10:30.412
oh yeah, I'm perfect just the way I am,

00:10:30.412 --> 00:10:32.024
and you don't change.

00:10:32.024 --> 00:10:33.866
And your life doesn't change,

00:10:33.866 --> 00:10:35.742
and nothing gets better for you,

00:10:35.742 --> 00:10:38.157
and you wonder what's happening.

00:10:38.157 --> 00:10:39.715
And everything is crumbling around you.

00:10:39.715 --> 00:10:41.668
Hey, I'm guilty.

00:10:41.668 --> 00:10:42.760
Terry Crews is guilty.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:44.764
Then I realize, wait a minute.

00:10:44.764 --> 00:10:45.597
Wait a minute.

00:10:45.597 --> 00:10:48.032
I'm not perfect just the way I am.

00:10:48.032 --> 00:10:50.124
I have to be perfected.

00:10:50.124 --> 00:10:53.837
I am perfect, but I am not complete.

00:10:53.837 --> 00:10:57.005
- At the end of the day, this
is called the music business.

00:10:57.005 --> 00:10:59.713
You've got to continue always find ways

00:10:59.713 --> 00:11:01.683
to better your business.

00:11:01.683 --> 00:11:04.333
It's kind of like Apple.

00:11:04.333 --> 00:11:05.791
You know, if he stayed

00:11:05.791 --> 00:11:08.138
on a laptop, then what would he be?

00:11:08.138 --> 00:11:08.971
No.

00:11:08.971 --> 00:11:11.462
What did Steve Jobs and the
whole team come up with?

00:11:11.462 --> 00:11:14.462
iTouch, iPad, iPod, now it's iCloud.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:17.504
It's always finding ways to get better,

00:11:17.504 --> 00:11:19.006
and that's the way I
looked at it through music.

00:11:19.006 --> 00:11:21.902
I said well, now I can
just go from rapping

00:11:21.902 --> 00:11:24.789
to everybody in the
neighborhood, to the whole world,

00:11:24.789 --> 00:11:26.135
and it worked.

00:11:26.135 --> 00:11:27.463
And now everybody's doing it.

00:11:27.463 --> 00:11:29.424
Now I got to figure out the next move.

00:11:29.424 --> 00:11:31.715
Now I got to figure
out my next iSomething.

00:11:31.715 --> 00:11:32.870
(laughs)

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:36.453
- I don't write down
goals, and go get 'em.

00:11:38.278 --> 00:11:41.265
I know a lot of people do
that, but I just kind of

00:11:41.265 --> 00:11:42.931
try to outdo myself.

00:11:42.931 --> 00:11:45.161
Outdo my last outdo.

00:11:45.161 --> 00:11:48.169
And although this is a
competition, in every sport,

00:11:48.169 --> 00:11:50.378
and I look at this industry has a sport,

00:11:50.378 --> 00:11:54.527
because it's numbers and it's
money, and it's achievements,

00:11:54.527 --> 00:11:58.717
and it's championships, and et
cetera, et cetera, you know.

00:11:58.717 --> 00:12:01.363
I'm only really in
competition with myself.

00:12:01.363 --> 00:12:04.122
You know, although it is,
you know, a fun competition

00:12:04.122 --> 00:12:05.852
to look over and see
what your homie doing,

00:12:05.852 --> 00:12:06.782
and see, you know what I mean?

00:12:06.782 --> 00:12:09.936
For me it's all about bettering me,

00:12:09.936 --> 00:12:12.759
bettering the music I make,
bettering my fan experience,

00:12:12.759 --> 00:12:16.926
bettering my business,
bettering myself as a man.

00:12:18.012 --> 00:12:19.269
The truth is, there's a lot I want

00:12:19.269 --> 00:12:22.028
to accomplish in the music industry.

00:12:22.028 --> 00:12:26.815
There's a lot I want to
accomplish as a businessman.

00:12:26.815 --> 00:12:28.567
I haven't done a lot of those things yet,

00:12:28.567 --> 00:12:31.194
although I have done a
lot, I feel like I'm just

00:12:31.194 --> 00:12:34.319
getting my feet wet and the
ceiling is very high for me.

